  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 157 PM MDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nTemperatures are much cooler this afternoon behind yesterday's cold \nfront (around 30 degrees cooler than they were at this time \nyesterday for the urban corridor and plains). Scattered showers \nare expected in the South Park and Palmer Divide areas this \nafternoon. Instability will be enough for an isolated thunderstorm\nor two as well, but severe weather is not expected. The lower \nlevels are still pretty dry, so our main concern this afternoon \nwill be virga and gusty winds. Overnight, there is a low chance \nfor a few showers and storms in our far northeastern plains, as \nsome sort of weak surface trough sets up and there's some low to \nmid level convergence. We'll see decent cooling overnight, with \nbelow-normal lows in the upper 40s to mid 50s across the plains \nin the morning.\n\nTomorrow, the warming trend begins again. The center of the upper\nlevel high will push north and slightly east each day as we go \ninto Friday. Highs on Wednesday will warm around 5 to 10 degrees \ncompared to Tuesday. On Thursday, models have a boundary draped \nacross the eastern part of our county warning area from north to \nsouth. Winds on the western side will be from the northwest and \nwinds on the eastern side will be from the southeast. There will \nalso be a significant moisture difference between the different \nsides of the boundary, with much lower dewpoint to its west. The \nweak downsloping and drier air will allow temperatures on the \nwest side of the boundary to increase into the mid 90s to around \n100 degrees. Whereas the east side of the boundary, could be more \nin the upper 80s to low 90s range. As for the smoke, the near \nsurface smoke from behind the cold front will continue to slowly \nclear out of the area over the next day or two. Aloft, the NW \nturning of the winds from the upper ridge building in, will give a\nbrief reprieve from the smoke moving in from Utah. However, with \ncontinued NW winds, smoke from the Pacific NW will likely be moved\ninto the area aloft. So, we may not see a complete clearing of \nthe smoke for a while.\n\nTemperatures for the weekend will continue to warm, as the ridge \nsits over the area. For Saturday and Sunday, portions of the \nplains around the I-25 and I-70 corridors have a medium to high \n(50-80%) chance of reaching the triple digits each afternoon (with\nlower chances in our southern counties). On Sunday, a weak upper \nlevel disturbance could move through the flow aloft, bringing the \nreturn of low end rain chances for the higher terrain.",
